# Exercise: How Many Arguments?
#
# Seq exposes command-line arguments through two words:
#
#   args.count   ( -- Int )       total number of arguments
#   args.at      ( Int -- String ) the argument at the given index
#
# Two things to know up front:
#
# 1. The argument at index 0 is the *program path itself*, not the
#    first user argument. So `args.count` for `myprog foo bar` is
#    3, and `0 args.at` is "myprog" (or its full path).
#
# 2. Under `seqc test` your program runs with no extra arguments,
#    so `args.count` is exactly 1 — the test binary's own path.
#    The assertions in this chapter are written against that fact.
#
# Your task: Write `user-arg-count` — the number of *user-supplied*
# arguments, i.e. `args.count` minus one for the program path.
#
# ## To see it on a real command line
#
# The seqlings test harness runs your code with no extra args, so
# the assertion below only ever exercises the zero-args case. Once
# you've solved it, drop this driver into the project root to play
# with the other counts:
#
#     ─── try-count.seq ─────────────────────────────
#     include "exercises/21-args/01-basics"
#
#     : main ( -- Int )
#         user-arg-count int->string io.write-line
#         0
#     ;
#     ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#     $ seqc build try-count.seq -o try-count
#     $ ./try-count                         → 0
#     $ ./try-count one                     → 1
#     $ ./try-count one two three           → 3
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: user-arg-count ( -- Int )
    # args.count - 1
    99
;

: test-no-user-args ( -- )
    # Under seqc test there are no user args, so the result is 0.
    user-arg-count
    # Do not edit below this line
    0 test.assert-eq
;
